1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
I think that there will be hope to find a solution.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:05,840
And I'll turn around.

3
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:09,640
So a mathematician, a friend of mine,

4
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:12,080
once told me a joke along the lines of,

5
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:14,360
a mathematician is stuck in a bank vault

6
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:17,080
with a raging out of control fire.

7
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:18,880
He's on the verge of being burned alive.

8
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:21,720
And then he lights a match, turns on the sink,

9
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:23,400
throws the match in, and says,

10
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,160
I've proven a solution exists.

11
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:31,720
Yes, so, but that sink isn't enough to go and put out

12
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:33,720
the fire, so what difference does it make?

13
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:37,840
It made a difference to him before he was burned alive.

14
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:41,960
I died knowing it could be solved.

15
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:48,000
So, now, if you're wondering, so why is this theorem so great?

16
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,560
So the philosophical perspective to think about

17
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:53,040
is, imagine you're in the 1950s, where

18
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:56,960
the main philosophical debate would be between a normal Paul

19
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:00,680
Samuelson Keynesian liberal and a Marxist.

20
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:02,800
This would be the main range of intellectual opinion

21
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,240
that you'd be getting during that time.

22
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:09,400
And so both of them agree that the distribution of resources

23
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:12,240
under capitalism is terrible and something ought to be done.

24
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:14,680
But the Marxist says, look, the problem

25
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:16,560
is inherent in the market itself.

26
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:19,400
We need to have communist revolution and markets

27
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:22,080
for all time, and then have the government go and ensure

28
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:24,720
a fair distribution forever.

29
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:27,840
And then, if you are a Keynesian liberal economist,

30
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:31,360
you whip out the second welfare theorem and say, wrong Marxist.

31
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:33,720
The real story is that markets are perfectly fine.

32
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:36,360
We just need to have massive redistribution once,

33
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:39,680
and then the free market is actually

34
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:41,960
going to give us just as egalitarian result as you want.

35
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:47,880
So within that framework of those

36
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:49,460
being the two sides that are arguing,

37
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:51,680
it's easier to see what the second welfare theorem is

38
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:52,180
doing.

39
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:54,720
It's shifting the blame from the market mechanism

40
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:56,280
to the endowment problem.

41
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:58,680
And just saying the problem is that endowments

42
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:03,800
were sufficiently equal, or actually, if they all

43
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:07,120
are, to some extent, they must be unequal in the right way.

44
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:10,320
So in particular, so if there are some people, for example,

45
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:15,560
who are, if there's someone who, I don't know what it could be,

46
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:18,440
someone who is just super low skilled.

47
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:20,200
Well, in that case, just equalizing

48
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:23,480
the regular physical commodities is not actually

49
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:26,160
going to equalize his life prospects, right?

50
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:27,800
Because suppose there's Bill Gates,

51
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:31,200
and then there's a guy who has $1 million for Bill Gates'

52
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:32,800
guilt.

53
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,480
So will it suffice just to go and take Bill Gates'

54
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:37,760
current possessions and then equalize them,

55
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:39,520
and then make sure that Bill Gates will

56
00:02:39,520 --> 00:02:41,400
have no more than this guy?

57
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,120
Will that do it?

58
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:44,600
What's going to happen?

59
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,320
Imagine you take all of Bill Gates' stuff

60
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:48,400
and give it to the guy who's not very skilled.

61
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:50,760
Does that mean, what is almost certainly going

62
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:54,680
to happen within a few hours?

63
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:58,240
Bill Gates is going to earn some income, which

64
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:01,000
will very quickly allow him to start gaining.

65
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,240
And if he works hard enough, he'll be able to regain.

66
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:04,680
So actually, what you really need

67
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:07,680
is not even just to equalize the initial endowments.

68
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:13,000
You need to equalize the ownership of labor and skill,

69
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,240
which means you would, like strictly speaking,

70
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:17,200
the second buffer theorem with production

71
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:20,000
would require that low-skilled people would

72
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,200
get to own Bill Gates, that Bill Gates would not

73
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:28,600
automatically get 100% ownership of Bill Gates,

74
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:32,040
but rather other people would get to own shares in him.

75
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:33,920
And then similarly, he could have said, well,

76
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:35,840
we'll compensate you by giving you some shares

77
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:36,760
and some janitors.

78
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:38,720
So can't I just be a free man?

79
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:40,720
No, because that's inconsistent with getting

80
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:42,560
equal life prospects for everybody,

81
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:45,080
because you are of super high skills,

82
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:46,560
and other people don't.

83
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:49,400
So the second buffer theorem, though people often

84
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:52,040
think of it as just a matter of we just sort of equalize

85
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:53,520
the physical stuff once.

86
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:55,480
But it goes way beyond that, because it

87
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:57,520
requires an equalization of really everything

88
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:00,680
in order to get that result.

89
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:01,180
All right.

90
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:03,440
So it sounds like Marxism has the win.

91
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:03,940
Yes.

92
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:05,040
Right, that did.

93
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:08,080
Well, if you think about it, Marxism is saying, look,

94
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:12,280
we need to also equalize labor, by saying that some people,

95
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:15,280
by saying that, no, you don't just get to go and own

96
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:16,440
your own labor.

97
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:18,120
We all are part of a community, and you

98
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:19,520
have to labor for the community.

99
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:20,840
Yeah.

100
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:21,760
All right.

101
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:24,040
Now, if you're wondering, so what does it really show?

102
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:27,560
Well, there are institutions that would actually

103
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:30,080
be self-reversing in a way that the second welfare theorem

104
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:31,520
shows markets are not.

105
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:33,720
So think about democracy, where we

106
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:36,600
have two wolves and a sheep voting over lunch.

107
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:38,000
Classic case.

108
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,320
All right, so you might say, well, look,

109
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:41,480
here's what we'll do.

110
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:43,600
So we don't like the democratic outcome.

111
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:45,960
Let's just go and take most of the wolves' assets

112
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:48,600
and just give them to the sheep, and that'll solve the problem,

113
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:50,040
right?

114
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:52,680
So every time there's an election,

115
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:54,080
and the wolves keep voting to say

116
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:55,840
the sheep has to do whatever we say,

117
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:58,880
and the sheep gets outvoted again and again and again,

118
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:00,320
and says the problem is democracy,

119
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:04,480
you say, no, let's just go and take most of the wolves' stuff

120
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:08,240
and give it to the sheep, and then that'll be fine, right?

121
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:10,480
And the answer is, no, that won't be fine.

122
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:11,960
Suppose you expropriate the wolves,

123
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:14,200
give everything they own to the sheep,

124
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:16,200
and then you have another election.

125
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:18,400
What are the wolves going to immediately vote to do?

126
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:19,480
Take all their stuff back.

127
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:20,680
Give back what you just took.

128
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:22,840
So that is a case where the institution of democracy

129
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:26,920
